# English captions (seconds in the ORIGINAL recording NAF009.mp3). *word* = shown in gold.
# The whole TurboScribe transcript (source/NAF009.srt) came back as Roman Urdu and was translated by hand: listen before
# publishing. Only the stretches used by the Shorts are captioned.
# Dropped by TurboScribe (retries pending, left out): 17:12-18:46, 23:28-23:47.
# Left out on purpose: the passage on the formless taking a likeness ("misali burqa", 7:38-9:04 and 16:40-17:12), which
# cannot be shown safely in a Short; the khilafat remarks (18:45-19:26); the landmarks on the way to the house
# (19:52-20:10); the line in the third sair about what is seen in the shaykh (26:24-26:27); the Persian couplet at 27:25.
# "Sarkar" = the Prophet ﷺ (at the Jali, 22:01) or the shaykh (Qadeer Pia Sarkar, Khwaja Mian Sarkar) by context.
CAPTIONS = [
    # --- Short 1: the purpose of Tasawwur-e-Shaykh
    (34.80, 47.40, "“The purpose of *Tasawwur-e-Shaykh*.” Bahr-ul-Uloom is telling us. Shaykh means the murshid: the one you have taken *bay'ah* with."),
    (49.70, 66.40, "“The purpose of Tasawwur-e-Shaykh”: holding the shaykh in your thought, thinking of his face, in imagination, in tasawwur, “is that the thought becomes fixed on *one point*.”"),
    (593.70, 614.60, "See, I read it again: “The purpose of Tasawwur-e-Shaykh is that the *thought* becomes fixed on one point.” The thought, there where we are doing tasawwur, is to be held on *one point*."),
    (614.60, 635.90, "That is: we are sitting in tasawwur of *Qadeer Pia*. Now you are not going anywhere else; your thought is not going anywhere else: it has become one point. Our thought is fixed on one point: only and only the tasawwur of Qadeer Pia *Sarkar*."),
    (635.90, 642.80, "“The purpose of Tasawwur-e-Shaykh is that the thought becomes fixed on *one point*.”"),
    # --- Short 2: Ya 'Aleemu, Ya Khabeeru (practice)
    (70.20, 79.10, "“The following *adhkar* also help:”"),
    (79.80, 101.60, "*Ya 'Aleemu* (O All-Knowing), Ya Khabeeru (O All-Aware), Ya Samee'u (O All-Hearing), *Ya Baseeru* (O All-Seeing)."),
    (102.60, 116.50, "“When you read *Ya 'Aleemu*, turn your attention toward the *umm-ud-dimagh*.”"),
    (117.00, 132.20, "The umm-ud-dimagh is above your palate. Umm-ud-dimagh: the *mother* of the brain; umm means mother. Its *root*, the place it comes from."),
    (133.30, 156.40, "“When you read Ya 'Aleemu, turn toward the umm-ud-dimagh, because that is the *centre of knowledge*, so that the knowledge of the *higher world* may settle in your heart.”"),
    (206.60, 216.80, "“When you read *Ya Khabeeru*, turn toward your *heart*, or toward the middle of the chest, so that you become aware of the whole world.”"),
    (217.20, 229.90, "Do you see? I tell you this again and again. Ya Khabeeru: O One who keeps *aware*, O One who gives news."),
    (230.60, 253.40, "“When you read Ya Khabeeru, turn toward your heart, or toward the *middle of the chest*”: toward your heart, or the space between your chest, “so that you become *aware* of the whole world.”"),
    (254.10, 260.80, "Allah will open to you the knowledge of the whole world; whatever is *needed*, He will keep opening it all."),
    # --- Short 3: Ya Samee'u, Ya Baseeru (practice)
    (265.20, 276.70, "“And when you read *Ya Samee'u*, turn your attention toward both your *ears*.”"),
    (277.40, 290.60, "“Your hearing will become *strong*”: whatever you hear will become very strong, “and you will hear what comes from the *higher world*.”"),
    (291.60, 306.60, "The higher world, *'aalam-e-'ulwi*: the loftiest station of all. What comes from there, you will hear."),
    (307.30, 315.10, "Do you see? “Not only this: your *outward* hearing too will become strong.”"),
    (315.10, 335.50, "So first: whatever voice comes from the higher world, you will begin to *hear* it; it will come to you. And whatever weakness there is in your ears' hearing, that too will become *strong*."),
    (336.00, 350.10, "“And when you read *Ya Baseeru*, press on the eyes and *open* them: you will see what you did not see until now.”"),
    (352.60, 362.00, "Ya Baseeru: O All-Seeing, O Giver of *insight*."),
    # --- Short 4: when 'aalam-e-misaal opened (the cycle at Yakutpura)
    (362.90, 377.50, "“And when you read *Ya Baseeru*, press on the eyes and open them: you will see what you did not *see* until now.”"),
    (390.50, 398.80, "See: he is saying that *kashf* opens. In kashf, Allah then shows whatever He wishes to show."),
    (399.90, 402.80, "What are its *effects*?"),
    (404.50, 420.60, "I was riding my *cycle*, out through the gate of *Yakutpura*, toward Itebar Chowk, toward Kali Kaman. I was on the road, reading: Ya 'Aleemu, Ya Khabeeru, Ya Samee'u, Ya Baseeru."),
    (421.00, 447.50, "Ya 'Aleemu, Ya Khabeeru, Ya Samee'u, Ya Baseeru. With it, this happened: the people were in view, the road was in view, I was going along the road, and from above every single thing was in view: *'aalam-e-misaal* had opened. Whatever was happening in 'aalam-e-misaal, I could *see* it all. Do you understand?"),
    (448.00, 457.50, "See what this is, what *knowledge* this is. All these people are blind; they do not know what any of it is."),
    (582.40, 593.40, "“Becoming aware of people's passing *thoughts*, and seeing faraway things: these are all matters of a *low degree*.”"),
    # --- Short 5: travelling in tasawwur (practice)
    (1168.90, 1179.50, "“Those linked to the *Siddiqi* line should close their eyes and, in tasawwur, come to *Malakpet*”: Malakpet, where Hazrat's house is."),
    (1180.30, 1188.80, "“On the way, whatever houses come, let them look at them all.” Eyes closed, in tasawwur, they are going along."),
    (1210.90, 1226.00, "“Let them look at them all; and whoever has *reach* as far as Qibla-e-Aalam, let him go on to *Qazipura*.” Qibla-e-Aalam is Khwaja Mian Sarkar. Whoever's reach goes that far, let him go to Qazipura."),
    (1227.30, 1235.50, "Understand: let him go to Qazipura, “and after *kissing the feet*, return.”"),
    (1236.10, 1246.30, "“The God-fearing and pious should go to *Makkah* al-Mukarramah, pray two rakats facing the *Ka'bah*, and return.”"),
    (1249.80, 1258.00, "There, the Ka'bah is before you; you are to pray two rakats as well."),
    (1258.20, 1277.50, "These are not *empty* words. Whoever has read this and is listening, present here: this command is being *applied* to all of them, to everyone present at this time."),
    (1279.30, 1294.80, "Then what worlds are going to open for you, what you will see, what you will hear: Allah knows best. This command is a place of great *honour*."),
    (1305.00, 1320.00, "“The *lovers* of Muhammad ﷺ should go to the musalla where the Prophet ﷺ used to pray, where there is a small arch, and picture that the Beloved of Allah ﷺ is *looking* from the blessed Jali.”"),
    (1321.90, 1326.30, "Picture this: that the *Prophet* ﷺ is looking. Do you see?"),
    (1329.30, 1335.80, "“Pray two rakats, and after the namaz recite *salat-o-salam*, and return.”"),
    (1598.90, 1616.00, "“Here Bahr-ul-Uloom means his *own* murids; his house is in Malakpet, Hyderabad. What is meant is: every person, when doing muraqaba, should picture his *own pir*, and go along the way to his residence.”"),
    # --- Short 6: the light like a search-light (practice)
    (1353.70, 1366.10, "“When fixing the tasawwur, think this: from *Allah*, a strong light, like a *search-light*, is reaching the blessed heart of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.”"),
    (1367.00, 1385.40, "A search-light. “When fixing the tasawwur, think this: from Allah, a strong light, like a search-light, is reaching the blessed *heart* of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.”"),
    (1386.30, 1396.70, "“And from there it is going into the *shaykh's heart*; and from the shaykh's heart it is reaching *my heart*.”"),
    (1396.80, 1408.50, "“Draw this light, with *full attention*, toward your own heart.”"),
    (1426.80, 1433.10, "Draw it toward the heart. “Do not take these as *mere imaginings*.”"),
    (1433.20, 1444.00, "“They are *bab-e-futuh*”: the door of opening, the door of success. Bab means *door*."),
    (1445.20, 1459.40, "“Do not take these as mere imaginings; they are bab-e-futuh. They make one *progress*, and show wondrous sights.”"),
    # --- Short 7: in the shaykh's form
    (1465.90, 1472.00, "“The thought grows *stronger*, and he begins to feel that he is in the shaykh's form.”"),
    (1473.10, 1488.50, "Now look: he progressed further, and he forgot *himself*. Now in whose form does he take himself to be? He is sitting in the form of his *murshid*."),
    (1489.50, 1505.20, "“He begins to feel: I am in the shaykh's form.” When he thinks of himself, he does not see himself: the *shaykh's* form is what he sees."),
    (1506.80, 1514.70, "“Even outwardly the effect is so great that in the murid's face a *glimpse* of the shaykh is seen.”"),
    (1515.70, 1533.20, "When the murid has gone this deep into tasawwur of his shaykh, little by little his face begins to *resemble* the shaykh's face."),
    (1534.20, 1541.90, "Other people too feel a glimpse of his shaykh in him, “and even the *voice* becomes alike.”"),
    (1543.00, 1550.70, "“If I say ‘I’, let only ‘*You*’ come from my tongue; whatever I look upon, let it be You I see.” *Hasrat*."),
    (1551.90, 1557.20, "Next is *sair*, the journey. Of how many kinds is it? Sair is of *three* kinds."),
    (1557.60, 1564.20, "“Number one: *sair fi'sh-shaykh*, the journey in the shaykh.”"),
    (1566.90, 1578.90, "“Number two: *sair lin-Nabi fi'sh-shaykh*. In this, through the shaykh's *mediation*, the light of the perfections of Muhammad ﷺ falls upon him.”"),
    (1580.10, 1583.30, "“Number three: *sair lillah fi'sh-shaykh*.”"),
    (1591.50, 1596.70, "“In this, through the shaykh's mediation, comes knowledge of the *tajalliyat-e-ilahi*, the divine manifestations.”"),
]
